<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tintagel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tintagel is a headland and village on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Excavations have found large quantities of imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> pottery and glass of the 5th and 6th centuries, showing that it was a high-status site, probably a royal centre of Dumnonia. In the 13th century Richard, Earl of Cornwall, built a castle there, and medieval legend made it the place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-arthur">King Arthur</a>'s conception.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tintagel.jpg" alt="Tintagel" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stuart Logan · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tintagel_Castle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2417082.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tintagel, also called Trevena, from the Cornish Tre war Venydh, meaning village on a mountain, is a civil parish and village on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>. The origin of the name is uncertain. It may be Norman French, or Cornish, and Oliver Padel proposes din, a fort, with tagell, meaning neck or narrow place. It first appears as Tintagol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>'s Historia Regum Britanniae, written about 1136. The coastline is of old Devonian slate, and the Tintagel slate quarries, worked from the fifteenth until the early twentieth century, are largely responsible for the jagged coast to the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> barrow in the parish stands at Condolden, and there were probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> fortifications at Willapark and Barras Head. A Roman milestone found in the churchyard wall in 1889 refers to the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a>. Major excavations beginning with C. A. Ralegh Radford in the 1930s showed that the headland was, in the 5th and 6th centuries, either a high-status Celtic monastery, as Radford held, or, according to later excavators, a princely fortress and trading settlement. Finds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> oil and wine jars show that Sub-Roman Britain was not as isolated as once thought. The Ravenna Cosmography, of around 700, mentions Purocoronavis, a fort of the Cornovii, which may have stood at Tintagel or at Carn Brea.</p><p>A small cliff castle was founded at Bossiney in Norman times, probably before the Domesday Survey of 1086. In 1253 Richard, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, established Bossiney and Trevena as a borough, and Tintagel was one of the seventeen ancient manors of the Duchy of Cornwall. From 1552 to 1832 the borough of Bossiney sent two members to the House of Commons.</p><p>In Geoffrey's account, Gorlois, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, placed his wife Igraine in Tintagol while he was at war. Merlin disguised Uther Pendragon as Gorlois so that Uther could enter and father a child, who was Arthur. This became the standard origin story of Arthur in later chronicles and romances, and Alfred, Lord Tennyson's Idylls of the King popularised the link in the modern era. Parts of the Tristan and Iseult legend are also set there. The Artognou stone, found in 1998, is not thought by historians to refer to Arthur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The parish had 1,725 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Tintagel Old Post Office dates from the 14th century and is a Grade I listed building owned by the National Trust. The Church of St Materiana, also Grade I, stands on the cliffs between Trevena and the castle, and its first church was probably of the 6th century. Tintagel has inspired composers, among them Arnold Bax, who wrote the symphonic poem Tintagel after a visit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tintagel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Imported Mediterranean pottery and glass at <b>Tintagel</b>, and at sites such as Bantham, show trade with the Byzantine world, probably for tin, in the 5th and 6th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dumnonia">Kingdom of Dumnonia</a></li></ul></section>
